# ------------------------------------------------------------------ discussion
H("Discussion")
P(f"In this retrospective data set of articles with deposited dates in Springer Nature and Wiley journals, acceptance that was unusually fast for the same journal and year was followed by retraction for peer-review-related reasons about four times as often as in the rest of the same journal-year. The relation decreased across the duration distribution, differed from the association with retractions that carry other reasons (ratio of odds ratios {f1(a21['ratio'])}), was present in a time-to-event analysis and within journal-months, and showed no clear association for a negative-control exposure. It agrees in direction with, and extends to individual articles, the editor-level and special-issue-level observations made for Hindawi and PLOS ONE ⟦bishop2023_hindawi_si⟧ and with the descriptive findings for retracted papers ⟦kocyigit2023_jkms,schonhaut2022_rpsp,sridharan2026_frma⟧. The journal-level null result of a recent study in this journal ⟦mascato2025_ripr⟧ is not in conflict, because our ecological analysis also found no positive relation for the share of very fast acceptances and the signal is relative to the journal's own norm. Reference ⟦richardson2025_pnas⟧ described short turnaround as a less robust marker than retractions or PubPeer comments at one large publisher; our estimates show that it can be informative but that its size varies by an order of magnitude between journals.")
P(f"The estimate must be read as a description of a historical episode rather than a stable law. The association was strong for articles accepted in 2021–2022 and not detectable in 2024; it differed between journals (prediction interval {f1(a16['pi_t'][0])}–{f1(a16['pi_t'][1])}); the stratified AUC of the time-ordered score fell from {f2(a18['auc_all']['auc'])} to {f2(a25['auc_without_largest_stratum'])} when one journal-year was removed; and most events come from a few journals. These features are what one would expect if compromised handling produced fast acceptance in bulk at some journals during a limited period, possibly in guest-edited collections, but article type and special-issue status are not recorded in these data, so this is a hypothesis. The decline in recent articles may reflect manipulation that no longer produces unusually fast acceptances, retractions that lag behind detection, or a change in what is retracted; screening rules should therefore be re-estimated rather than fixed.")
P(f"The size of the effect and its practical value are different questions. The odds ratio is large, but retractions are rare, so flagged articles are overwhelmingly not retracted: in the fastest 1% of the score about one article in {1/sc['pit_le_0.01']['ppv']:.0f} was later retracted for process-related reasons, and the score identified a small share of such retractions. Timing is therefore not a detector of misconduct. The group-level analysis suggests a more useful framing: journal-months in which at least 15% of articles were in the fastest 5% had a retraction rate about {f1(a26['rate_ratio_flagged_vs_other']['est'])} times that of other journal-months, with {pc(a26['flag_fast_ge_15pct']['share_articles'], 1)} of articles in the flagged months, which is the kind of signal editorial offices can use to prioritise a collection or an editor's decisions for review, together with other signals; it was not validated prospectively and its interval is wide. A companion tool in the repository computes within-journal-year percentiles, batch days and group-level alerts and states these limits. Ascertainment deserves emphasis: publishers that screen by turnaround and then retract will generate an association even if turnaround says nothing about the quality of review. Our analysis of publisher-investigated retractions with other reasons (odds ratio {f2(a21['or_pubother'])}) and of retractions without the investigation label gives some reassurance but is small, so the findings should be read as an association between fast acceptance and publisher-detected process failures, which is what screening tools are asked to predict, not as independent proof that fast acceptance indicates inadequate review.")
P(f"Three points of method may help later work. Pooling across journals and years, or comparing retracted articles with all others, would confound speed with journal practice and follow-up; the ecological association of very fast articles and retraction even has the opposite sign to the within-journal one. Cohort construction matters: a cleaning rule that looked neutral removed 28% of retracted articles and was found only by an independent re-analysis, and an even larger share of retractions never entered the cohort because the articles lack deposited received and accepted dates. And because few journals contribute most events, an association such as that for batch acceptance can disappear when mass-retraction events are removed, so conclusions should be checked for concentration. Journals can repeat the analysis on their own submission data, where handling-editor and article-type information would sharpen it.")
H("Strengths and limitations")
P(f"Strengths are the size of the cohort ({BS['n_articles']:,} articles, {BS['n_retracted']:,} retractions), the comparison within journal-year strata, a protocol that predates the analysis with amendments disclosed, a negative-control exposure and a reason-specific outcome, a time-to-event and a group-level analysis, adversarial review that found and led to the correction of a cohort error, and public code and data. The study is observational: associations are conditional on journal and year and do not show that fast acceptance caused inadequate review, which we cannot observe; article type, special-issue status and handling editor are not available. Retraction is a late, incomplete and publisher-dependent marker; unretracted problematic articles are counted as controls, which would bias the odds ratio towards one if they are also fast, whereas publishers' use of timing in their own investigations would bias it away from one; Retraction Watch labels are revised over time ⟦singh2026_rw_revisions⟧ and the snapshot does not record when each notice entered the database.")
P(f"The cohort contains only articles with deposited received and accepted dates, and the share of retractions with such dates differs between journals (Supplementary Table S1), so the cohort is a selected part of all retractions; the association was present in subsets with high and low coverage but its size varied (Table 3). Three publishers deposit these dates, Taylor & Francis contributed {T['by_member']['Taylor & Francis']['retracted']} retractions, and the Blackwell imprint showed no association, so the results speak for part of two publishers; the external replication had too few events. The effective sample is {a1['R_process']['joint_fe_logit']['n_strata']} informative journal-years in {a1['R_process']['joint_fe_logit']['n_clusters']} journals; cluster intervals rest on few influential clusters. The stratified AUC rests on a small number of outcome-selected journal-years and on a score whose cohort-shift variance was estimated on the evaluated cohorts (with negligible effect), the evaluation is retrospective and time-ordered rather than a prospective test, and rates in recent years are lower bounds because of follow-up. Duration is publisher-defined and for transferred or resubmitted manuscripts may not measure the editorial process; 37% of articles have only a month-level issue date instead of a true first-online date, which affects the visibility rule of the score and the lag analysis, which was restricted to true dates; reference lists of retracted records are unreliable, so reference counts were not used. Only two tests were primary and six amendments, five of them responding to results, are disclosed and should be read as exploratory; the protocol has no external time stamp. The analysis and first drafts were produced by an AI system with automated reviews; the authors have re-examined the results, and the code and data are available for independent replication.")
H("Conclusions")
P("Acceptance that is unusually fast relative to the same journal and year was associated with later retraction for peer-review-related reasons in this retrospective data set, specifically for process-related reasons and in time-to-event and journal-month analyses, but the association varied widely between journals and years, was concentrated in 2021–2022 and a limited number of journals, and could not be separated from possible publisher-led ascertainment. As an article-level screen it offers modest enrichment at low yield. Journal-relative timing may help editorial offices prioritise groups of articles, such as journal-months or collections, for review; it must not be used to judge individual papers, and any rule should be re-estimated routinely.")
H("Abbreviations")
P("MH, Mantel–Haenszel; OR, odds ratio; HR, hazard ratio; CI, confidence interval; PIT, probability integral transform; AUC, area under the receiver-operating-characteristic curve; X1–X5, L1–L2, N1, exposure definitions given in Methods; RECORD, REporting of studies Conducted using Observational Routinely-collected health Data.", size=10)
